                  Stainless Runtime Library Exception
                            Version 1.0

An additional permission under section 7 of the GNU General Public
License, version 3, granted by the copyright holders of Stainless.

NOTE ON THIS TEXT
-----------------

This wording was drafted for Stainless rather than copied from an
existing instrument. It follows the same shape and intent as the GNU GCC
Runtime Library Exception, version 3.1, which serves the same purpose for
GCC, and which you may wish to read alongside it:

    https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gcc-exception-3.1.html

If the licensing of this project matters commercially to you, have this
text reviewed by someone qualified, or replace it with a published
instrument such as the GCC Runtime Library Exception.


0. DEFINITIONS
--------------

"Stainless" means the compiler and associated tooling in this repository.

"Runtime Library" means the files in the runtime/ and stdlib/ directories
of this repository, together with any object code or other compiled form
of them that Stainless places into, or links with, the code it produces.

"Independent Module" means source code that is neither part of the
Runtime Library nor derived from it.

"Target Code" means output produced by Stainless from one or more
Independent Modules, together with any part of the Runtime Library that
Stainless included in, or linked with, that output.

"Eligible Compilation Process" means a process that produces Target Code
from Independent Modules using Stainless, or using other software
licensed under the GNU General Public License version 3 or a licence
compatible with it.


1. GRANT OF ADDITIONAL PERMISSION
---------------------------------

You have permission to propagate Target Code, and to convey it under
terms of your choosing, even where doing so would otherwise be restricted
by the GNU General Public License, provided that all of the Target Code
was produced by an Eligible Compilation Process.

This permission attaches to the Runtime Library only. It does not extend
to Stainless itself, nor to any modified version of the Runtime Library:
conveying either of those remains subject to the GNU General Public
License, version 3, in full.


2. WHAT THIS MEANS IN PRACTICE
------------------------------

Compiling your own program with Stainless does not place your program
under the GNU General Public License. You may licence what you write
however you wish, and distribute it in binary form, even though the
Stainless runtime is linked into it.

Modifying Stainless, or modifying the Runtime Library, and distributing
the result to others does place obligations on you: the modified source
must be made available under the GNU General Public License, version 3.

In short, the copyleft is on the compiler, not on what you build with it.
